RESULT_PARAGRAPHS = {
    "p108": "To define the cellular composition of the hepatopancreatic sinus (Hs) and its response to hemospasia, we performed single-nucleus RNA sequencing on Hs tissue from control and 6-h hemospasia groups. A total of 19,795 nuclear barcodes were identified, and 9,658 nuclei were retained after doublet and quality filtering (5,349 control and 4,309 hemospasia; Table S8, Table S9). t-SNE separated the nuclei into 10 transcriptional states: PH, GH, TS, MH2, F, R, B, M, MyoEpi, and PM-like (Fig. 5C,D), each with a distinguishable marker-gene profile (Table S10).",
    "p109": "Hemospasia produced a state-selective reorganization of the Hs nuclear landscape (Fig. 5G; Table S12). PH increased from 6.8% to 15.7% (2.30-fold), R from 2.2% to 5.1% (2.29-fold), and MH2 from 5.8% to 12.6% (2.17-fold), whereas PM-like decreased from 10.9% to 2.9% (0.27-fold) and TS from 52.1% to 40.6% (0.78-fold). These proportion estimates are exploratory because each condition is represented by one pooled library; their value lies in identifying which states are selectively mobilized rather than in estimating between-animal variance.",
    "trajectory": "To relate these discrete transcriptional labels to a continuous state framework, we overlaid a candidate state-differentiation trajectory on the Hs t-SNE (Fig. 5F). The trajectory connected PH, biosynthetic transition, GH and MH2 populations, suggesting that PH-related cells may move through biosynthetic intermediate states toward GH- and MH2-like fates. The arrows summarize relative transcriptional relationships and do not constitute lineage tracing; the trajectory therefore provides a hypothesis for how the observed states are connected rather than a direct map of cell fate.",
    "comm1": "Because the state shift after hemospasia could reflect altered local signaling, we examined SonicParanoid-supported ligand-receptor combinations among Hs states (Fig. S2A-D; Table S16, Table S17). In the ECM-integrin axis, MyoEpi-to-PH and MyoEpi-to-GH communication had the highest strengths (0.24 and 0.25), compared with 0.14 for TS, 0.15 for M, and 0.11 for MH2. At the individual ligand-receptor level, VWF-ITGB1 contributed most to the MyoEpi-to-PH/GH signal (0.178 and 0.181; state-label permutation P < 0.001). THBS4-ITGB1, JAG1-NOTCH1 and POSTN-ITGB1 also showed significant but weaker candidate communication from M to PH/GH. ITGB1 expression was sparse in PH and GH nuclei (positive fractions 7.6% and 8.4%; median expression 0; Table S18), identifying this axis as a candidate matrix-adhesion mechanism rather than evidence of strong receptor activation.",
    "comm2": "Comparison of control and hemospasia conditions revealed a directional rearrangement of candidate communication sources. The VWF-ITGB1 probability from MyoEpi to PH increased from 0.129 to 0.287 (log2FC = 1.16, 500-permutation P = 0.028), while the corresponding MyoEpi-to-GH change was 0.153 to 0.263 (log2FC = 0.78, P = 0.068). M-derived THBS4-ITGB1 estimates decreased, but these pair-level changes were not significant under the same permutation test (P = 0.60 for M-to-PH and P = 0.35 for M-to-GH). Together, these candidate shifts link PH mobilization to a reweighting of local matrix-associated signals.",
    "bridge": "Collectively, these analyses define Hs as a dynamic multi-state landscape in which PH occupies a central junction between progenitor-like, biosynthetic intermediate and differentiated hemocyte states. Whole-tissue profiles, however, cannot resolve whether PH expansion reflects a uniform proliferative burst or a coordinated shift within PH itself. This distinction is central to defining the cellular basis of Hs regeneration and motivates the state-level analysis of PH in the following section.",
    "p111": "Cell-cycle analysis showed that PH nuclei had high proliferative potential: their G2/M fraction was 46.2% (n = 1,044), the highest among well-populated states, whereas the G2/M fractions of GH, TS, MH2, F, R, B and PM-like ranged from 25.0% to 33.9% (Fig. 6A). M nuclei had a G2/M fraction of 53.3% but comprised only 45 nuclei. PH nuclei were further resolved into four transcriptional substates, s0-s3 (Fig. 6B; Table S11): s0 was ECM/ribosome-high, s1 was progenitor-like, s2 was immune/ECM-primed, and s3 was TGM1-low. The substates had distinguishable marker combinations: s0 was enriched for PEN-3 and ECM-related transcripts; s1 expressed progenitor-like markers including Igfbp7 and ccnd2; s2 was enriched for THBS4, TGM1 and LOC113810024; and s3 expressed LOC113818748, LOC113823090 and LOC113823089 with low TGM1 expression (Fig. S1A; Table S11).",
    "p112": "After hemospasia, PH expansion was accompanied by a shift in substate composition: s0 decreased from 48.1% to 12.4%, s1 increased from 24.9% to 38.3%, s2 increased from 7.9% to 31.7%, and s3 changed from 19.1% to 17.6%. Genes related to progenitor maintenance, ECM organization and cell maturation changed continuously along PH pseudotime rather than forming completely separated expression blocks (Fig. 6C; Table S14). In the PH-only t-SNE, s1 localized mainly on the left, s3 mainly on the right, s0 in the center and s2 in an intermediate transition region (Fig. 6D). The s1-s0-s2-s3 line connects substate centroids to summarize transcriptional continuity and does not represent lineage tracing. State-level control-versus-hemospasia DEGs are reported in Table S13.",
    "external": "To test whether the progenitor-like identity of PH extended beyond Hs, we scored the external LyO progenitor top-50 program across states (Fig. 6E; Fig. S1C,D; Table S15). The score was highest in PH (mean UCell-style score 0.525), with 77% of PH nuclei above the global median; the remaining states had mean scores of 0.469-0.502, with 20%-60% of nuclei above the global median. The LyO progenitor program and the PH top-50 marker set shared four genes (66.9-fold enrichment, P = 3.7 x 10^-7, BH-adjusted P = 1.9 x 10^-6), whereas the other examined state marker sets had zero overlap. This represents program-level concordance between PH and the external LyO progenitor program.",
    "identity": "To separate progenitor identity from cell-cycle activity, we compared the PH2 identity score with the cell-cycle score across Hs nuclei (Fig. S1B). PH nuclei were generally above the background cell-cycle-identity trend, and the mean adjusted identity residual was 1.14 z units (95% CI 1.10-1.18; Fig. 6F). We then compared Hs PH with hemolymph PH. The raw PH2 score was higher in hemolymph PH (0.887 versus 0.840), but its cell-cycle percentile was also much higher (0.975 versus 0.568). After cell-cycle adjustment, the residual was 1.14 (95% CI 1.10-1.18) for Hs PH and -0.13 (95% CI -0.15 to -0.10) for hemolymph PH, a difference of 1.27 (95% CI 1.22-1.31). Hs PH therefore retains a stronger progenitor-identity program beyond the cell-cycle background than hemolymph PH.",
}
